WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:03.200
I’m often asked
what my opinion

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.760
about a diet
or disease is.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.080
Who cares what my or
anyone else’s opinion is.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:16.160
All we should care about
is what the science says.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.240
What does the best available
balance of evidence

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.240
Published in the peer-
reviewed medical literature

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.680
have to say right now?

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.960
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.520
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.840
Did you know that a single
meal high in saturated fat

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.520
can impair artery function
for hours after consumption?

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.760
Here’s our first story.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.480
We are only as old as our arteries.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.920
What can we do to preserve
artery function as we age?

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:50.160
A poor diet and sedentary behavior
can lead to adverse aging processes,

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.520
like impairment of the little power
plants in our cells, which can result

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.520
in free radical formation, oxidative
stress, and inflammation, which leads

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:03.440
to artery dysfunction that can end in
the cardiovascular disease that ends us.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.040
In a series of videos I did
about a decade ago,

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.400
I discussed this landmark research
showing that a single high-fat meal

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.960
could cripple artery function
within hours of consumption,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.760
compared to no change
in the low-fat meal.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.120
The high-fat meal that so
crippled artery function included

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.280
Sausage and Egg McMuffins
from McDonald's.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.040
How do we know the sausage,
egg, or cheese was to blame?

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.360
What about the crappy
carbs in the biscuits?

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.040
We know because the low-fat meal
that didn't impair artery function

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.160
was a sugary mess
of carby Frosted Flakes.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.880
And just when your artery
function finally starts to recover

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.680
five or six hours later?
Lunch time,

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.080
when your arteries may get
whacked with another load

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.840
of meat, eggs, dairy, or oil.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.920
Why does it matter so much what
happens after a meal within your body?

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.120
Because most of us spend about 16
hours a day in that after-a-meal state,

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.440
constantly hammering our arteries.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.360
No wonder cardiovascular
disease is our #1 killer.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.160
And it doesn't just inflame the arteries
in our heart, but our lungs as well.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.840
A high-fat challenge increases
airway inflammation

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.960
and impairs bronchodilator
recovery in asthma.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.120
Have asthmatics cough up sputum
from their lungs four hours

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.240
after the same kind of high-fat
meal and inflammatory cells

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.160
shoot up in the high-fat meal group.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.840
In terms of lung function, give
them two hits of their inhalers,

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.480
containing a drug called albuterol or
Ventolin, and their airways open up

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.720
as they should after the low-fat meal.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.440
But after the high-fat meal, the same
inhaler doses don't work as well,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.880
crapping out after a few hours
because of all the extra inflammation

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:41.920
in their lungs.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.960
What you eat can determine
how well you breathe.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.240
Okay, but that was asthmatics.
But even in nonasthmatic subjects,

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.480
you get the same spike
in inflammatory cells

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:55.920
in sputum coughed out of your lungs
four hours after eating what was,

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.200
in this case, a Jimmy Dean's
Meat Lovers breakfast bowl.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.360
And there aren't only more
inflammatory cells; there's also

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.080
a doubling of the amount of
pro-inflammatory oxidized bad

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.040
LDL cholesterol sucked up
by the type of white blood cells

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.400
that go on to form foam cells.
Those are the cells that build up

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.920
the inflamed pus in your artery
wall that leads to heart attacks.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.680
And all this happens within just
hours of eating pizza in this case.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.560
The fat in your blood goes up
and so do your endotoxin levels.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.760
Endotoxins are components
of bacterial cell walls,

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.360
and foods like meat can be so
contaminated with bacteria—

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.800
alive and dead—that they
accumulate endotoxins,

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.120
and we're talking about both
red meat and white meat.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.760
But recent research published in
2020 suggests the main culprit

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.960
may not be endotoxins
after all, but the fat itself.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:48.960
The saturated fat floating in your
blood after an unhealthy meal

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.800
may be inducing the inflammation
more directly. Either way,

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.360
we are accountable for
what we eat meal-by-meal

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.840
for the modification of the risk factors
for chronic metabolic disease.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:04.320
In our next story, we look
at the window of time

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:07.840
in which sufficient physical
activity can help mediate

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:11.120
some of the damage caused
by eating an unhealthy meal.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.520
In my last video, I discussed
studies that show a single meal

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.280
high in saturated fat can impair artery
function in men, as measured in the arm,

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.600
but what we're more concerned about
is blood flow to the wall of the heart.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:27.440
Researchers randomized men to
eat either a high-fat meal that was

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:31.520
more than 60% fat, half of it saturated,
with more than an egg's worth

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.400
of cholesterol, or a low-fat
meal that was mostly carbs

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.520
with less than 10% fat and
50 times less cholesterol.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.960
If part of a coronary artery
is blocked for any reason,

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.440
the surrounding vessels expand.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.720
That extra expansion capacity is
called the coronary flow reserve,

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.560
and it's clamped down within
hours of eating a fatty meal,

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:54.000
undermining the heart's ability to
compensate for clogged arteries.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.200
That's how a high-fat meal
affects blood flow to the heart.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.360
What happens if you exercise, though,
right after that high-fat meal?

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:02.640
After-the-meal inflammation

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.680
following the prolonged elevation
of fat in the blood that occurs

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:06.800
when you eat high-fat meals,

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.320
provides a likely explanation for
increased cardiovascular disease.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:14.320
But substantial evidence has
shown that acute exercise is

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.520
an effective modality for clearing
out some of that fat after a meal.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.600
However, the benefits of acute
exercise for postprandial lipemia,

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.680
for after-the-meal fatty blood,
appear to be relatively short-lived.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.520
Going a few days without exercising
may completely negate any benefit,

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:30.800
no matter how fit you are.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.400
The time window appears to be
between 18 hours before the meal,

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.880
up until around 90 minutes
after the meal.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:38.800
And how much exercise do we need?

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.480
About an hour of moderate-intensity
exercise should do it.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.480
In this study though, it only took
20 minutes of stair climbing,

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.040
broken up into five minutes
every hour for four hours

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.240
after a McDonald's breakfast
of hash browns, eggs, pancakes,

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:54.240
muffins, sausage, and a milkshake.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.200
Following such a meal, artery
function significantly decreased

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.520
when the subjects just
sat around after eating,

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.360
but not when they did the
hourly stair-climbing exercises.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:07.440
In conclusion, hourly exercise may
attenuate the negative effects

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:10.560
not only of prolonged sitting but
also of high-fat meal intake,

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.040
suggesting that stair climbing
should be incorporated

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:17.360
as an easily accessible lifestyle
strategy to protect artery function.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.560
Of course, it goes without saying
that the other way you can

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:24.160
protect artery-function is not
to eat breakfast at McDonald's

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:24.800
in the first place.

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.360
Such a meal would
also have more than

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.600
2,000 mg of sodium—more than the
1,500 the American Heart Association

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:33.840
recommends you stay
under for the entire day.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.040
Give someone a meal with
even less salt, a third less,

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.440
and that alone can still impair artery
function within an hour of consumption,

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:44.000
even independent of the
increase in blood pressure.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:47.520
When it comes to blood pressure,
some people are salt-sensitive,

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:50.240
meaning they suffer a large bump
in blood pressure when they eat salt.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.400
But others are said to be salt-resistant.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:55.760
Their blood pressure doesn't really
depend much on their salt intake.

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:58.000
So for these people is salt okay?

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:03.280
No. High dietary sodium intake
reduces artery function regardless

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.320
of whether your blood pressure
is salt-sensitive or salt-resistant.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:08.720
Your artery function
is impaired either way,

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:11.280
going from a low salt diet
to a high salt diet.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:15.840
See, there's an influence of dietary
salt beyond blood pressure.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:20.480
In spite of the seemingly unanimous
consensus, some researchers—

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:23.520
too often funded by the salt industry—
claim that it's actually not good

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:27.840
to cut down on salt, but the evidence
is against these dissenters.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.000
Like the saturated fat
in meat, dairy, and junk,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.040
the science indicates that sodium,
not sodium reduction,

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:34.560
is the real villain.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:39.680
Finally today, if you’re going
to eat something unhealthy,

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:42.960
is there anything you
can pair it with

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:46.196
to help mediate the
damage it may cause?

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.920
If you compare the artery function
of those who don't eat meat

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:53.120
to those who do, the healthy
ability of arteries to dilate and

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:57.200
let more blood flow is significantly
better among those eating vegetarian.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:00.120
And not just by a little.
We're talking four times better.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:04.960
Well, duh, vegetarians tend to be
younger, smoke less, be slimmer,

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:07.680
and have lower rates of diabetes,
high cholesterol, high blood pressure,

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:11.440
and heart disease. But the
researchers controlled for all that.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:14.720
They only let healthy
nonsmokers into the study,

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:18.240
and recruited a group of meat eaters
who were just about as slim,

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:20.640
on average, and had about
the same blood pressures,

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:23.040
even practically the
same cholesterol—

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.440
a really healthy cohort
of omnivores—

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:29.760
yet they still got their arteries
handed to them by the vegetarians.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:32.640
And the longer someone
was meat-free, the better.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:36.080
The degree of superior artery
function correlated

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:37.760
with the number of years
eating meat-free.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:41.440
Instead of their artery function
worsening over time as they aged,

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:44.160
it got better the longer
they ate that way.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:47.760
This suggests that vegetarian
diets, by themselves,

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.280
have a direct beneficial effect
on artery function and may help

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:52.640
to account for the lower incidence

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:55.520
of atherosclerosis and
cardiovascular mortality.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:59.520
Since researchers were able to
control for other known risk factors,

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:04.160
they figured it must be the food.
But what aspect of the food?

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:07.520
Is it simply the lack of the
deleterious effect of meat?

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:10.720
Or could it also be because the
vegetarians may simply be eating

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:12.880
more whole healthy
plant foods, for example,

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:14.880
up to a serving a day
more vegetables.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:19.520
When researchers compared two crappy
meals, Sausage and Egg McMuffins

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:23.280
to Frosted Flakes, and found the
fatty meal impaired artery function

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:27.040
within hours but the sugary meal
didn't, they blamed the fat.

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:30.480
But it may just be the animal fat,

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:34.240
since high-fat whole plant foods
like nuts don't have the same effect.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:36.240
In fact, if you look at
a systematic review

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:38.320
of all the randomized
controlled trials on the effect

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:40.480
of nut consumption
on artery function,

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:43.520
you find that nuts actually
make things better over time.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.160
Enough to counter the artery-crippling
effects of a salami sandwich?

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:54.320
Let's find out. And the answer is
yes for walnuts, but no for almonds.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:57.280
Just like there are some fruits
that are better than others,

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:00.240
like blueberries over bananas,
there are some nuts that are better

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.320
than others, and walnuts appear
to be the blueberries of nuts.

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:08.000
What about the blueberry of
berries: blueberries themselves?

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.600
A randomized, controlled crossover
trial of cooked blueberries,

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:14.160
raw blueberries, or
no blueberries at all.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:17.840
If you feed people buns made out
of white flour, eggs, butter, and salt,

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:20.080
and fill them mostly
full of sugar and eggs,

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:23.280
you get a gradual drop in artery
function over the next six hours.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:27.280
But add the equivalent of a cup of
wild blueberries to that same bun,

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:30.080
and instead you get a big
boost in artery function,

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:33.040
almost as if you had just mixed
the blueberries with water.

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:38.080
About the same amount of strawberries
failed to rescue artery function

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:41.760
from the likes of two cheese blintzes
with whipped cream, a sugary syrup,

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:46.160
egg and bacon, but that is
quite a heavy load to bear.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:50.240
What about acai berries versus a
meal with a similar amount of fat?

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:54.480
One and a half-frozen smoothie packs
with half a small banana in water

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.520
were able to significantly
rehabilitate arterial function

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:00.800
compared to a control smoothie
with the same banana and water

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:02.720
colored to look like the acai one,

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:05.520
though obviously it would
have tasted differently.

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:08.560
This group of researchers went
all out, though, and performed

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:12.240
a double-blind randomized
controlled trial with raspberries,

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:16.000
measuring artery function after
2 hours and then 24 hours after

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.320
drinking about 3/4 of a cup of frozen
red raspberries blended with water,

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:22.160
or about a cup and a half,

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:26.480
versus a placebo drink meant
to match both color and taste.

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:31.600
The fake berry drink had no effect on
artery function, but the other two did.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:35.680
Note the 3/4 cup dose
seemed to work just as well

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:38.960
as the cup and a half dose, which
is what you see with blueberries:

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:41.840
the benefits plateau
after about a cup.

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:46.240
The bottom line is that consumption
of dietarily achievable amounts of

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:49.840
red raspberries acutely improves
artery function for up to 24 hours.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:53.760
You say, "Yeah, but by the end of the
day you're only up like one percent."

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:58.160
Ah, but at a population level, each
one percent increase is associated

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:01.520
with a 12% reduction in risk
of a cardiovascular event

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:06.000
like a heart attack or stroke, all
from just having a berry smoothie.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:08.320
What about berry juice?

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:11.760
Five different concentrations
of cranberry juice were used,

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:16.240
along with a placebo control evidently
indistinguishable in color and taste.

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:20.000
The 25% cranberry juice drink
gave a little bump at two hours;

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.800
the 50% juice was still
working eight hours later.

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:28.080
The 75% juice, the one that was nearly
pure juice, and the ultra-concentrated

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:32.080
juice also improved artery function
within hours of consumption.

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:36.400
But this, like that last raspberry
study, just involved straight berries

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:38.400
without some artery-crushing meal.

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:42.080
Would berry juice be able
to stop artery dysfunction

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:44.720
caused by a high-fat
meal this unhealthy,

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:46.880
squeezing down artery
function within hours?

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:52.240
Researchers created a cocktail
of grapes, lingonberry, blueberry,

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:54.907
strawberry, and black aronia berry
and were able to turn this

00:12:54.907 --> 00:12:57.600
into this. No significant change
after the high-fat meal.

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:00.080
Of course, if you had just
drunk those berries alone,

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:02.640
you'd probably get an improvement,
but it's better than nothing.

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:08.800
Well, what about something a little
less exotic than black aronia berries.

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:10.240
What about OJ?

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:13.360
Participants were provided a high-fat
meal of ham and cheese croissants,

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:17.680
along with either a cup of water,
orange juice, green tea, or red wine.

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:22.960
The arteries didn't much like the
croissants, and OJ was useless,

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:28.000
as was the green tea and red wine.
So it's probably best to not eat

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:30.160
ham and cheese croissants
in the first place.

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:32.800
In fact, drinking orange juice
with a fatty meal

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:34.960
could actually make things worse.

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:38.640
If you give people bacon and cheese
muffins with or without orange juice,

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:42.800
the OJ can lead to a prolongation
of elevated fat in the blood

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:45.920
as your body preferentially burns
for energy all the rapidly absorbed

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:50.160
free sugars in the juice, meaning
sugars not encased in cells walls,

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:52.279
as in those in whole fruit.

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:55.760
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:59.040
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.600
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:05.360
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:09.040
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:12.320
please go to the Nutrition Facts
Podcast landing page.

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:14.880
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:19.040
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite to each of these topics.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:23.200
For a timely text on the
pathogens that cause pandemics,

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:26.160
order the e-book,
audiobook, or hard copy

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:28.960
of my last book,
“How to Survive a Pandemic”.

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:33.120
For recipes, check out
my second-to-last book,

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:35.440
my “How Not to Diet Cookbook”.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:38.640
It’s beautifully designed
with more than 100 recipes

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:40.720
for delicious and
nutritious meals.

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:43.360
And all the proceeds
I receive from the sales

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:45.840
of all my books goes to charity.

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:49.920
NutritionFacts.org is a nonprofit,
science-based public service,

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.080
where you can sign up
for free daily updates

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:56.320
on the latest in nutrition research
via bite-sized videos and articles.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.360
Everything on the
website is free.

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:01.680
There’s no ads, no
corporate sponsorship.

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:04.800
It’s strictly non-commercial.
I’m not selling anything.

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:08.240
I just put it up as a public service,
as a labor of love,

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:11.040
as a tribute to my grandmother,

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:25.840
whose own life was saved
with evidence-based nutrition.

